{
  "summary": "You identify key ethical issues and stakeholders in the INTRODUCTION, and your discussion leads to a clear decision to prioritise safety. To strengthen the report, use a concrete risk-based decision process, correct the numerical and Volkswagen-case errors, and make your recommendation conditional on fixing defects and completing verification. Adding the missing abstract and improving references and presentation will also help.",
  "next_steps": [
    "In “DICUSSION: To Ship or Not to Ship,” replace the incorrect estimate: 1% of 5,000 servers is 50, not 500. Assess each defect by its likelihood and severity, explain what level of risk is acceptable, and use that assessment to decide whether shipment can proceed.",
    "In “The Most Ethical Path” and “RECOMMENDATION,” accurately describe the Volkswagen case as diesel-emissions testing, then state plainly that Rachel should accept the contract only if defects are corrected and adequate verification is completed. Clarify what the proposed product-cycle condition means.",
    "Add an abstract, improve the completeness and consistency of your citations in “References,” and consider a relevant table or figure to present the risk assessment or stakeholder impacts."
  ],
  "criteria": {
    "C1": "Your INTRODUCTION identifies safety, commercial pressure, professional responsibility, and stakeholders including Rachel, her company, the chip contractor, and the health department. This meets the Excellent level; sustain this strength by making each stakeholder’s distinct interests explicit throughout the report.",
    "C2": "Your discussion presents arguments for and against shipping, and “The Most Ethical Path” prioritises potential harm to users. To reach Very good, develop objective arguments in greater depth and draw decision recommendations for several stakeholders, using a risk-based assessment rather than speculative benefits.",
    "C3": "The pro/con structure in “DICUSSION: To Ship or Not to Ship” supports your conclusion that not shipping is the ethical choice. To reach Very good, make the argument more compelling by connecting each claim to evidence and explicitly resolving when shipment could become acceptable after defects are addressed.",
    "C4": "You use an engineering ethics code, NHS IT-failure evidence, external cases, and a quantitative estimate. To reach Very good, substantiate claims with adequate, consistently presented references and relevant qualitative or quantitative evidence; correct the 1% calculation and the Volkswagen case description.",
    "C5": "Your report has an introduction, body, conclusion, and references, and remains generally readable. To reach Good, add the missing abstract, use a relevant visual element to support the discussion, and improve language and citation consistency while retaining relevant references."
  }
}
